> In article <rfz_0G_@engin.umich.edu>, oliveria@engin.umich.edu (Roque Donizete de Oliveira) writes:
>    > I'm using a decstation5000 running Ultrix 4.2 with DEC Fortran
>    > version 3.04 and dbx version 2.10.1.
>    > I've compiled the file below as
>    >                                 f77 -g2 -O0 junk.f
>    > then I ran 
>    >             dbx a.out
>    > 
>    > The problem is that it seems the DEC Fortran compiler optimizes away
>    > the PARAMETER declaration. I want the debugger to know about
>    > variables in the PARAMETER statement (so I can examine them within
>    > the debugger). Is there any of accomplishing this (through other 
>    > compilation flags or perhaps a newer version of DEC Fortran) ?
>    > 
>    > C-----------------------------junk.f-----------------------------
>    >       PARAMETER (R=1.2)
>    >       print *,'Enter x'
>    >       read (*,*) x
>    >       y=sin(x*r)
>    >       stop
>    >       end
>    > C----------------------------------------------------------------
>    > 
> 
> PARAMETER declarations aren't variables, they are pretty much like the
> #define in C but more limited, i.e. really just handled at a textual
> level. The compiler just replaces the R with 1.2, then compiles the program.
> I doubt if any compiler will propagate the names in a parameter statement
> to debug. You would need to replace it with an actual variable R, possibly
> filled by a DATA statement to get the same effect. Then youshould
> be able to see it in a debugger.
> 
> Bear in mind that since PARAMETER values are fixed constants, they
> can't change, so you shouldn't really need to look at them with a
> debugger.
> 
> 
> -- 
