In article <14113@bunny.GTE.COM> dhp1@gte.com (Dave Pascoe) writes:

>I have been given the use of a MicroVAX-II with a single 70M drive.
>It was running Ultrix 3.2 (I think) and I tried upgrading to Ultrix 4.2
>but obviously didn't have enough disk space to do it.
>My goal is to have the minimal setup necessary to provide a mail and
>news gateway to a few users.

	You can do all this, but it requires a fair bit of fiddling. The
best way to do it (IMHO) is to zap the machine, partition the disks,
bring it up under the standalone in-memory shell, make the filesystems,
and then restore just the parts you need from a dump archive taken
from a machine that is already installed the way you want it. In
such a case, you'll want to pick and choose carefully. Plan to do your
kernel builds someplace else, so leave /usr/sys off, /usr/include off,
/usr/lib gets stripped down to just /usr/lib/dnet, and so on. It can
take a while to get it all right. The way I've done this in the past
is to just set up 2 partitions: swap and root. Then I restore a minimal
root filesystem (there goes 15Mb) which I then strip down some after I
boot it - after that, just make multiple passes through a dump archive
of /usr, restoring what is needed. To speed this up, in the past I have
done stuff like make the archive on a large fileserver, then mount it
via NFS and restore from the archive file. Restore is still too stupid
to know when it's on a seekable device, so on a uVAXII, this will take
some time and a lot of coffee.

mjr.
