In article <60640@netnews.upenn.edu>, ross@cattell.psych.upenn.edu (Ross Porter) writes:
> We're trying to speed file xfer to a uVAX II (KA630) console port.
> Increasing from 9600bps to 19200bps has had only a small effect
> on the xfer rate.  Is this indicative of the console port hardware, 
> or should I look elsewhere for a bottleneck?

  All of the VAX console ports are "funny" in one respect or another. As a
bare minimum, they're interrupt-per-character, and are sometimes part of
another CPU (like the LSI-11 on a VAX-11/785, the Pro on an 8530, etc.). If
you need high-speed transfers, I'd suggest a DHQ11 card, or a DHV11. If
you have Ethernet but no direct terminals, a terminal server port would work
as well.

