> Increasing from 9600bps to 19200bps has had only a small effect
> on the xfer rate.  Is this indicative of the console port hardware, 
> or should I look elsewhere for a bottleneck?

It is probably indicative of the console port hardware, and how it must be
accessed by the operating system.  All VAX processors' console ports that I've
looked at are extremely primitive, and the KA630's is no exception.  Would you
believe an interrupt per character in or out, with no silo either way (at least
I can't find any mention of one), and of course no DMA capability?  In fact, 
the programming interface looks a lot like the old DL11...

Try to buy a DHV or CX or a terminal server or something like that -- your
KA630 will be glad you did. 

I say "probably indicative" because you may have a bottleneck elsewhere as
well. Incoming uucp transfers, for example, at 1400 char/sec measured
throughput, use from 70% to 80% of a MicroVAX II's CPU (this is with VMS,
DECUS uucp, and a DHV11 mux).  I would not count on being able to shove 
19.2Kbits/second into the KA630's console port and have the CPU be available
for much of anything else at the time.  

